# a-base-on-two-fronts — plan 14 H2's baseline, and B7's ending twice.
#
# `a-tower-buries-its-own-wall.keys` mirrored: two spawns, two towers,
# two walls, one core.  Nobody clears up.
#
#   this file — nobody:               77 ticks   ( 51 s)
#   one helper, east front:          214 ticks   (143 s)
#   one helper, west front:          211 ticks   (141 s)
#   TWO helpers, both on the east:   214 ticks   — worth nothing
#   one helper on each front:        242 ticks   (161 s)
#
# ⚠ **The ending is plan 12 B7's, on both sides at once.**  This base
# falls with both of its walls at 97 of 100 — barely scratched — and a
# 1.5 m body ramp standing at (6, 0) and (-6, 0).  Nobody had to break
# anything: each tower killed its attackers at the foot of its own
# wall, and the survivors walked over their dead.
#
# ── ⚠ Why the walls run r = -4..4 over a band that is only -2..2 ──
#
# Not decoration, and not a wider seal — the hexes outside the band are
# sea, so nothing walks there either way.  It is what makes the two
# fronts COMPARABLE, and the first attempt at this base showed why it
# had to be done: with the walls stopping at the band's edge, one
# helper east read 112 ticks and one helper west 211, on a base that
# looks mirror-symmetric.
#
# The cause is plan 12 B3 and not the crew.  A wall's END is worth 30%
# of a braced hex, the siege chews where the ROUTE meets the wall, and
# odd-r rows are offset — so the eastern approach fan happened to
# include an end hex and the western one did not.  One front was
# breaking a 30 HP hex while the other chewed 100 HP.
#
# Extending both walls two hexes past the walkable band makes every
# REACHABLE wall hex fully braced, and the two fronts come back to
# within three ticks of each other (214 against 211).  ⚠ So a base's
# two sides are only as alike as their BRACING, and that is a property
# of the map a scenario has to control for before it can measure
# anything else.

echo a base on two fronts: two towers, two ramps, and nobody clearing up

do toggle_mode

do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

# One tower a front, each one hex behind the wall it covers (B5b).
do cycle_kind
click 3 0
marker 3 0 tower
click -3 0
marker -3 0 tower

# Two spawns, pointed at each other across the core.
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 11 0
marker 11 0 spawn 3
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click -11 0
marker -11 0 spawn 0

count markers 5 5
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -12 -2 12 -2
drag -12 -1 12 -1
drag -12 0 12 0
drag -12 1 12 1
drag -12 2 12 2
count painted 125 125

# ⚠ Both walls run past the band on purpose — see the header.
palette 9
click 5 -2
click 5 -1
click 5 0
click 5 1
click 5 2
click 5 -4
click 5 -3
click 5 3
click 5 4
click -5 -2
click -5 -1
click -5 0
click -5 1
click -5 2
click -5 -4
click -5 -3
click -5 3
click -5 4

# Every reachable wall hex is braced to the full 100, both sides.
kind 5 0 wall
kind -5 0 wall
hp 5 0 100.0 100.0
hp -5 0 100.0 100.0
hp 5 -2 100.0 100.0
hp -5 -2 100.0 100.0

# A wave round-robins over the active spawns, so ten is five a side.
wave 10
count alive 10 10
wallet 200.0 200.0

tick 22
wave 16

# ⚠⚠ **132, and plan 24 W1 made it LONGER rather than shorter** — 123
# before it.  The wider siege front is not only a way of breaking more
# wall: a besieger that stops at the hex it is touching is a besieger
# that is NOT walking on to stand on the core, and the wallet is drained
# by nibblers rather than by damage.  So the same rule that opens a
# perimeter faster also holds robots off the thing that ends the run.
fall 800
ticks 128 138
wallet 0.0 0.0

# ── ⚠⚠ What got them in — and plan 16 W2 MOVED it ───────────────
#
# Before the pre-walk window this base fell in 77 ticks with three dead
# robots piled 1.5 m at the foot of each wall: B7's *a tower buries its
# own wall*, arriving twice.  With the window it falls in 123, and the
# bodies are somewhere else.
#
# ⚠ **The window relocates the tower's kills to the SPAWN MARKER.**  A
# wave stands 8 ticks where it appeared, stacked, inside a 15-hex tower
# range — so the first three deaths on each front happen out there, and
# 1.5 m of robot piles up on a hex that leads nowhere.
pile 11 0 1.4 1.6
pile -11 0 1.4 1.6

# ⚠ **What is left at the wall is 1.0 m — and 1.0 m is EXACTLY enough.**
# A 3.0 m wall less a 2.0 m climb is a 1.0 m step, so two dead robots
# still get the third one over.  The ramp did not stop forming; it got
# thin, and it moved a row north.
pile -6 -1 0.9 1.1
pile 6 0 0.0 0.0

# The west wall is intact where they crossed it — they walked over it,
# not through it — while the east front spent the run chewing.
#
# ⚠ **And since plan 24 W1 the east front chewed THROUGH.**  It used to
# finish the run with a quarter of its HP left; the front is the wall
# FACE's width now, so the same robots spread across the face and open
# it.  ⚠ The contrast with the west is what this file is for and it is
# untouched: one front walked over its wall on a body ramp, the other
# came through it.
kind -5 -1 wall
kind 5 0 grass
hp -5 -1 95.0 100.0

snap h2_a_base_on_two_fronts
